Here is an example of a SystemVerilog module for an LDPC encoder for FPGA implementation:

```verilog
module ldpc_encoder(
    input wire clk,
    input wire reset,
    input wire data_in,
    output wire data_out,
    parameter integer WIDTH = 8,
    parameter integer NUM_PARITY = 4
);

reg [WIDTH-1:0] data_reg;
reg [NUM_PARITY-1:0] parity_reg;

always @(posedge clk or posedge reset)
begin
    if (reset)
    begin
        data_reg <= 0;
        parity_reg <= 0;
    end
    else
    begin
        data_reg <= data_in;
        parity_reg <= calculate_parity(data_reg);
        data_out <= generate_output(data_reg, parity_reg);
    end
end

function [WIDTH-1:0] calculate_parity(input wire [WIDTH-1:0] data);
    // Implementation of the LDPC parity calculation algorithm
endfunction

function [WIDTH-1:0] generate_output(input wire [WIDTH-1:0] data, input wire [NUM_PARITY-1:0] parity);
    // Implementation of the LDPC output generation algorithm
endfunction

endmodule
```

This module has a clock (clk), a reset (reset), an input data signal (data_in), and an output data signal (data_out). The module also has parameters for the width of the data and the number of parity bits. The module uses registers to store the input data and the parity information, and it has always blocks to update the registers based on the clock and reset signals. The module also has functions to calculate the parity and generate the output based on the input data and parity information.

