In the dining philosophers problem, deadlock can occur when all philosophers try to acquire the chopsticks one at a time, leading to a situation where no philosopher can continue eating. The four necessary conditions for deadlock in this setting are:

1. Mutual exclusion: Only one philosopher can use the chopsticks at a time.
2. Hold and wait: A philosopher who has already acquired one chopstick will wait for the other philosopher to release their chopstick before attempting to acquire the second chopstick.
3. No preemption: Once a philosopher has acquired a chopstick, they cannot be forced to release it.
4. Circular wait: There exists a sequence of philosophers such that each philosopher is waiting for the next philosopher in the sequence to release their chopstick.

To avoid deadlocks, we can eliminate any one of the four conditions. For example, we can implement a "round-robin" strategy where each philosopher takes turns using the chopsticks, without holding and waiting for others. This ensures that no philosopher is blocked from acquiring the chopsticks, and therefore prevents deadlocks.

Here's some pseudo code for a round-robin strategy:

```
function round_robin(philosophers, chopsticks) {
    // Initialize a turn counter
    let turn = 0;

    // Main loop
    while (true) {
        // Get the current philosopher
        let current_philosopher = philosophers[turn];

        // Acquire the chopsticks for the current philosopher
        current_philosopher.acquire_chopsticks();

        // Increment the turn counter
        turn++;

        // Check if all philosophers have had a turn
        if (turn >= philosophers.length) {
            // Reset the turn counter
            turn = 0;
        }
    }
}
```

