To implement Auth0 SSO for multiple React apps, follow these steps:

1. Sign up for an Auth0 account and create a new application.
2. In the Auth0 dashboard, navigate to the "Applications" tab and click on your newly created application.
3. Click on the "Settings" tab and copy the "Domain" and "Client ID" values.
4. In each of your React apps, install the `@auth0/auth0-react` package using npm or yarn:
```bash
npm install @auth0/auth0-react
```
5. Create a new file called `auth0.js` in your React app's root directory and add the following code:
```javascript
import { Auth0Provider } from '@auth0/auth0-react';

const auth0 = new Auth0Provider({
  domain: 'YOUR_DOMAIN',
  clientId: 'YOUR_CLIENT_ID',
});

export default auth0;
```
Replace `YOUR_DOMAIN` and `YOUR_CLIENT_ID` with the values you copied from the Auth0 dashboard.
6. Wrap your app component with the `Auth0Provider`:
```javascript
import React from 'react';
import auth0 from './auth0';

function App() {
  return (
    <Auth0Provider {...auth0}>
      {/* Your app content goes here */}
    </Auth0Provider>
  );
}

export default App;
```
7. Now, you can use the `useAuth0` hook from the `@auth0/auth0-react` package to authenticate users in your React apps. For example:
```javascript
import { useAuth0 } from '@auth0/auth0-react';

function LoginButton() {
  const { loginWithRedirect } = useAuth0();

  return (
    <button onClick={() => loginWithRedirect()}>Login</button>
  );
}

export default LoginButton;
```
That's it! With these steps, you have implemented Auth0 SSO for multiple React apps. Users will be able to log in once and access all your applications seamlessly.

Here's a simple C function that converts an array of command-line arguments (`argv`) to a null-terminated string:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

char *convert_argv_to_string(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    int i;
    size_t len = 0;
    char *str = NULL;

    for (i = 0; i < argc; i++) {
        len += strlen(argv[i]) + 1; // Add one for the null terminator
    }

    str = malloc(len);
    if (!str) {
        return NULL;
    }

    i = 0;
    while (*argv[i] != '\0') {
        str[i] = *argv[i];
        i++;
    }
    str[i] = '\0';

    return str;
}

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    char *str = convert_argv_to_string(argc, argv);
    if (str) {
        printf("Converted string: %s\n", str);
        free(str);
    } else {
        printf("Error allocating memory.\n");
    }

    return 0;
}
```

This function takes two arguments: `argc` (the number of command-line arguments) and `argv` (an array of pointers to the command-line arguments). It allocates memory for a null-terminated string, iterates over the `argv` array, and copies each argument to the string. Finally, it returns the converted string.

