Here's the decorator you requested:
```typescript
import { Injectable, CanActivate, ExecutionContext } from '@nestjs/common';

@Injectable()
export class EnvDecorator implements CanActivate {
  canActivate(context: ExecutionContext): boolean {
    const request = context.switchToHttp().getRequest();
    const nodeEnv = process.env.NODE_ENV;

    if (nodeEnv === 'true') {
      return true;
    } else {
      return false;
    }
  }
}
```
This decorator checks the `NODE_ENV` environment variable and returns `true` if it's set to "true", otherwise it returns `false`. You can use this decorator in your Nest.js controllers or services to restrict access based on the environment.

For example, you can use this decorator to protect an API endpoint that should only be accessible in production:
```typescript
import { Controller, Get, UseGuards } from '@nestjs/common';
import { EnvDecorator } from './env.decorator';

@Controller('api/protected')
export class ProtectedController {
  @Get()
  @UseGuards(EnvDecorator)
  getProtectedData(): string {
    // Only execute this code if NODE_ENV is set to "true"
    return 'Protected data';
  }
}
```
In this example, the `getProtectedData` method will only be executed if the `NODE_ENV` environment variable is set to "true". Otherwise, the method will return an error.

