Here's a simple React utility to remove widows. This component takes in a paragraph and removes any lines that are too short, ensuring that no word is left alone on a line by itself.

```jsx
import React from 'react';

const RemoveWidows = ({ paragraph }) => {
  const lines = paragraph.split('\n');
  const newLines = lines.map((line) => {
    const words = line.split(' ');
    const lastWordIndex = words.length - 1;
    const lastWordLength = words[lastWordIndex].length;

    if (lastWordLength > 0 && words[lastWordIndex] !== '') {
      return line;
    } else {
      return '';
    }
  });

  return (
    <div>
      {newLines.join('\n')}
    </div>
  );
};

export default RemoveWidows;
```

To use this component, simply pass in the paragraph you want to remove widows from:

```jsx
<RemoveWidows paragraph="This is a sample paragraph with some words that are too short to be left alone on a line." />
```

This will output the modified paragraph with any lines that are too short removed:

```
This is a sample paragraph with some words that are too short to be left alone on a line.
```

