```powershell
# Define function to check if SID is in SIDHistory
function IsSidInSIDHistory($sid) {
    $domain = (Get-ADDomain).Name
    $sidHistory = Get-ADUser -Filter "samAccountName -eq '$sid'" -Properties sidHistory | Select-Object -ExpandProperty sidHistory
    return ($sidHistory -contains $sid)
}

# Define function to check if folder ACLs refer to SIDs in SIDHistory
function CheckFolderAcls($folderPath) {
    $acl = Get-Acl -Path $folderPath
    foreach ($access in $acl.Access) {
        if ($access.IdentityReference -match '^S-1-5-21-') {
            $sid = New-Object System.Security.Principal.SecurityIdentifier($access.IdentityReference)
            if (IsSidInSIDHistory($sid)) {
                Write-Host "Folder $folderPath has an ACL that refers to a SID in SIDHistory: $sid"
            }
        }
    }
}

# Recursively check folders for ACLs referring to SIDs in SIDHistory
function CheckFoldersRecursively($startPath) {
    Get-ChildItem -Path $startPath -Directory | ForEach-Object {
        CheckFolderAcls($_.FullName)
        CheckFoldersRecursively($_.FullName)
    }
}

# Start checking folders from root directory
CheckFoldersRecursively("C:\")
```

This script defines two functions: `IsSidInSIDHistory` and `CheckFolderAcls`. The first function checks if a given SID is present in the SIDHistory of any user or group in the domain. The second function checks if the ACLs of a given folder refer to any SIDs in SIDHistory. The third function recursively checks all folders in the root directory and its subdirectories for ACLs referring to SIDs in SIDHistory.
